prompt_sent:
You are the Formatter skill. You are the conventional TERMINAL node of
every DAG. Your job is to produce the final user-facing answer from
whatever upstream nodes have provided.

You make no tool calls. The user's original query appears under
USER_QUERY. Upstream results appear under INPUTS.

Procedure:
  1. Read USER_QUERY.
  2. Read INPUTS and decide which fields / findings answer the query.
  3. Write the user-facing answer in plain English. Adapt the format
     (numbered list, comparison table, one paragraph) to what the
     question actually asked.

Output schema (JSON, no prose, no markdown fences):

  {
    "final_answer": "<the answer the user sees>"
  }

Rules:
  - This is the LAST node. Do not add successors.
  - The answer must be answerable from INPUTS alone. If an upstream
    node returned `(not found)` or marked itself failed, say so plainly
    to the user rather than inventing.
  - Cite sources only when an upstream node included them (Researcher
    nodes do; Retriever nodes do). Do not invent URLs.

USER_QUERY: Find the populations of Tokyo, Delhi, and Shanghai, and tell me which two cities have populations that differ by less than 5% of each other.

MEMORY HITS (4 from FAISS):
  - [fact] Current population and growth rate inquiry for Lagos, Cairo, and Kinshasa
      source: user_query
      raw: For Lagos, Cairo, and Kinshasa, find current populations and growth rates and tell me which is growing fastest.
  - [fact] Current population and growth rate query for Lagos, Cairo, and Kinshasa
      source: user_query
      raw: For Lagos, Cairo, and Kinshasa, find current populations and growth rates and tell me which is growing fastest.
  - [tool_outcome] Fetch Claude Shannon wikipedia page for birth date, death date, and three information theory contributions
      source: user_query
      raw: Fetch https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claude_Shannon and tell me his birth date, death date, and three key contributions to information theory.
  - [tool_outcome] Fetch Claude Shannon wikipedia page for birth date, death date, and three information theory contributions
      source: user_query
      raw: Fetch https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claude_Shannon and tell me his birth date, death date, and three key contributions to information theory.

INPUTS:
[
  {
    "id": "USER_QUERY",
    "kind": "query",
    "value": "Find the populations of Tokyo, Delhi, and Shanghai, and tell me which two cities have populations that differ by less than 5% of each other."
  },
  {
    "id": "n:2",
    "kind": "upstream",
    "skill": "researcher",
    "output": {
      "question": "What is the current population of Tokyo?",
      "sources": [
        {
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tokyo",
          "title": "Tokyo - Wikipedia"
        },
        {
          "url": "https://www.nippon.com/en/japan-data/h02639/",
          "title": "Tokyo Third in UN Ranking of Global Megacities at 33.4 Million"
        }
      ],
      "findings": "The population of Tokyo varies significantly depending on whether the scope is the city proper (Tokyo Metropolis) or the Greater Tokyo Area.\n\nAs of 2025, the population of Tokyo Metropolis (the city proper) is estimated to be over 14 million residents.\n\nThe Greater Tokyo Area, which includes the surrounding prefectures and constitutes one of the world's largest metropolitan regions, has an estimated population of approximately 33.4 million people as of 2025."
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "n:3",
    "kind": "upstream",
    "skill": "researcher",
    "output": {
      "question": "What is the current population of Delhi?",
      "sources": [
        {
          "url": "https://worldpopulationreview.com/cities/india/delhi",
          "title": "Delhi Population 2026"
        },
        {
          "url": "https://statisticstimes.com/demographics/india/delhi-population.php",
          "title": "Delhi population 2026 - StatisticsTimes.com"
        }
      ],
      "findings": "Estimates for the population of Delhi vary depending on the definition of the administrative area (such as the city proper versus the National Capital Territory or the wider National Capital Region) and the specific demographic model used.\n\nWorld Population Review estimates the 2026 population of Delhi at approximately 23.39 million, noting a steady annual growth rate of roughly 2.42%. Other projections, such as those cited by StatisticsTimes, provide varying figures for the National Capital Territory, with 2025 estimates often ranging between 22 million and 23 million.\n\nIt is important to distinguish between different geographical definitions. The National Capital Territory (NCT) of Delhi is distinct from the larger National Capital Region (NCR), which encompasses surrounding satellite cities in neighboring states and has a significantly higher total population, exceeding 50 million according to older census data and recent estimates."
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "n:4",
    "kind": "upstream",
    "skill": "researcher",
    "output": {
      "question": "What is the current population of Shanghai?",
      "sources": [
        {
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shanghai",
          "title": "Shanghai - Wikipedia"
        },
        {
          "url": "https://www.ceicdata.com/en/china/population-prefecture-level-city/population-shanghai-usual-residence",
          "title": "Population: Shanghai: Usual Residence | Economic Indicators | CEIC"
        },
        {
          "url": "https://www.macrotrends.net/global-metrics/cities/20656/shanghai/population",
          "title": "Shanghai, China Metro Area Population (1950-2026) | MacroTrends"
        }
      ],
      "findings": "The population of Shanghai varies depending on the definition of the city boundaries (administrative vs. metropolitan area) and the methodology used (usual residence vs. urban area estimates). \n\nOfficial data for the usual resident population of the Shanghai administrative area was reported at approximately 24.85 million in 2025 by the Shanghai Municipal Bureau of Statistics. \n\nAlternative estimates that account for the broader metropolitan area, which includes surrounding urbanized regions, often report significantly higher figures, with some sources citing over 30 million people as of 2025."
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "n:5",
    "kind": "upstream",
    "skill": "coder",
    "output": {
      "code": "populations = {\n    \"Tokyo (Metropolis)\": 14.0,\n    \"Tokyo (Greater Area)\": 33.4,\n    \"Delhi (NCT/WPR)\": 23.39,\n    \"Shanghai (Administrative)\": 24.85\n}\n\nnames = list(populations.keys())\nfor i in range(len(names)):\n    for j in range(i + 1, len(names)):\n        p1, p2 = populations[names[i]], populations[names[j]]\n        diff = abs(p1 - p2)\n        avg = (p1 + p2) / 2\n        percent_diff = (diff / avg) * 100\n        print(f\"{names[i]} vs {names[j]}: {percent_diff:.2f}% difference\")",
      "summary": "The code calculates the percentage difference between all pairs of the provided population figures: Tokyo Metropolis (14.0M), Greater Tokyo Area (33.4M), Delhi (23.39M), and Shanghai (24.85M). The analysis shows that Delhi (23.39M) and Shanghai (24.85M) differ by approximately 6.05%, while no other pair falls below the 5% threshold. Therefore, no two cities in this dataset differ by less than 5%."
    }
  }
]
